@node Main "GLBench"

@{b}GLBench 0.1@{ub} -- a stress benchmark for minigl.library

GLBench draws seven scenes, each leaning on another part of the 3D card,
and gives the card a score. Five of them keep their shapes on the card
and leave it the work; two measure the bus between the Amiga and the card. Together the scenes use every feature
RadeonGL, the minigl.library for the ATI Radeon 8500 to 9250, offers.
Burn runs one scene for ten minutes and checks all the while that the
picture stays right.

    @{" Running it                           " link Running}
    @{" The seven scenes                     " link Scenes}
    @{" The score                            " link Score}
    @{" Burn                                 " link Burn}
    @{" From a Shell                         " link Shell}
    @{" What you need                        " link Requirements}
    @{" History                              " link History}
@endnode

@node Running "Running it"
@toc Main
@next Scenes

@{b}Running it@{ub}

Double-click @{b}GLBench@{ub}. It opens a 640 x 480 screen and draws the
seven scenes in turn, 400 frames each: two to three minutes on a 68060
with a Radeon 9200. A line at the bottom shows the scene, the frame and
the frames a second. Esc ends the run, the space bar skips a scene (a
skipped scene gets no score).

At the end the result stays on the screen for a minute, or until a key:
each scene's frames a second, and the score. The same table goes to the
window GLBench was started from and to @{b}RAM:GLBench-Result.txt@{ub},
with the machine, the library and the screen mode.

Every scene draws its frames by number, not by the clock: each frame
moves the picture on by the same step, so every machine draws the same
frames, and the figures compare.
@endnode

@node Scenes "The seven scenes"
@toc Main
@prev Running
@next Score

@{b}The seven scenes@{ub}

@{b}1 Pelt@{ub}
    A furred ring turning in a breeze: the ring drawn 20 times, each a
    little larger, the strands let through by the alpha test, blended,
    from two textures at once. It presses on the fill rate: the burner.

@{b}2 Lanterns@{ub}
    Eight coloured lanterns swinging in front of a waving banner, in fog.
    The banner's wave is computed once and kept on the card. Local lights,
    spot lights and a directional one, both sides of the banner lit, the
    lit pass added over an ambient one. It presses on the card's transform
    and lighting.

@{b}3 Chrome@{ub}
    Chrome bodies on plinths circling over a striped floor: a sphere, a
    ring and a vase kept on the card, mirrored with a sphere map; plinths,
    labels and a floating sphere from display lists. Texture coordinate
    generation, display lists, polygon offset.

@{b}4 Embers@{ub}
    A storm of soft glowing sparks with trails, the screen cut in four,
    each quarter blending another way: added, subtracted, the darker, the
    brighter. Blending, points in three sizes, wide lines.

@{b}5 Plasma@{ub}
    A wall of twelve tiles: a plasma sent to the card every frame, a
    palette that turns, every texture format and every way a texture
    meets the colour beneath it. Texture uploads.

@{b}6 Swarm@{ub}
    A flock of 1920 darts round cubes and dodecahedra, kept on the card in
    eight groups that turn at their own pace. The last third is drawn
    twice, through a red and a cyan mask, for red/cyan glasses. Geometry:
    the card transforms and lights 23,040 corners a frame.

@{b}7 Mirror@{ub}
    A picture that falls into itself: every frame is read back from the
    card and drawn again, smaller and turned. The bus, both ways.
@endnode

@node Score "The score"
@toc Main
@prev Scenes
@next Burn

@{b}The score@{ub}

Each scene's frames a second are set against a reference machine's: an
A4000 with a 68060 and a Radeon 9200 behind a Mediator, at 640 x 480 x 16.
The score is the geometric mean of the seven ratios, times 1000. So the
reference machine scores 1000, a machine twice as fast in every scene
2000, and one slow scene cannot hide behind a fast one.

The score compares only at the same screen size and depth: the log
prints the mode. Without all seven scenes in full, the score is marked
"not comparable".

In version 0.1 the reference figures are provisional (the score says
so); they will be measured on the reference machine.

The table also gives million primitives a second (triangles, lines and
points) and, for Plasma and Mirror, the megabytes a second they move over
the bus themselves.
@endnode

@node Burn "Burn"
@toc Main
@prev Score
@next Shell

@{b}Burn@{ub}

Double-click @{b}GLBench-Burn@{ub}. It draws Pelt for ten minutes. Every
250 frames it draws one fixed frame, reads the middle of it back and
compares it with the first time: a card that runs too hot, or a bus that
loses data, shows up here as a picture error, with its frame number.
At the end: the frames a second on average, the slowest and the fastest
five seconds, the checks and the errors. Esc ends it sooner.

The Radeon has no temperature sensor, and GLBench reads none. Mirror's
frames depend on the ones before, so Burn cannot check it.
@endnode

@node Shell "From a Shell"
@toc Main
@prev Burn
@next Requirements

@{b}From a Shell@{ub}

    GLBench [SCENE=<n,...>] [FRAMES=<n>] [BURN=<minutes>] [SHELLS=<n>]
            [WIDTH=<w>] [HEIGHT=<h>] [DEPTH=16|32] [WINDOW]
            [LEFT=<x>] [TOP=<y>] [NOHUD] [HOLD=<s>] [LOG=<file>]
            [SHOT=<file>] [SHOTS=<drawer>]

SCENE        the scenes to run, numbers or names: SCENE=1,3 or
             SCENE=pelt,chrome (all seven)
FRAMES       frames a scene (400)
BURN         Burn for so many minutes; BURN=0 until Esc. The scene is the
             first of SCENE (Pelt)
SHELLS       Pelt's shells, 1 to 64 (20)
WIDTH, HEIGHT, DEPTH   the screen (640 x 480 x 16)
WINDOW       in a window on the Workbench instead, at LEFT, TOP
NOHUD        no line at the bottom
HOLD         seconds the result stays on the screen (60; 0 not at all)
LOG          the result file (RAM:GLBench-Result.txt)
SHOT         the result screen as a PPM picture
SHOTS        a drawer for a PPM picture of every scene's middle frame

C/GLBench is for the 68040 and 68060, C/GLBench030 for the 68020 and
68030 with a 68881 or 68882; the two icons choose by themselves.
Return codes: 0 done; 5 done, with a GL error or a picture error; 20
could not run.
@endnode

@node Requirements "What you need"
@toc Main
@prev Shell
@next History

@{b}What you need@{ub}

- A minigl.library 29.1. GLBench is made for RadeonGL (0.12 or later
  recommended) and uses its static-array extension when the library
  names it; with another minigl.library it runs without it.
- A 68020 or better with an FPU (68881, 68882, or the 68040's or
  68060's own).
- AmigaOS 3.1 or later.
@endnode

